From 186c78e60f71d155199a02a4aa9b4e72742130de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 19:27:10 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: mediatek: set the port PHY up before sys_ck on EN7528

The "pcie" clock prepare callback of the EN7528/EN751221 SCU driver ends by
deasserting PERST#.  mtk_pcie_enable_port() prepares that clock before the
port PHY is initialised, so the endpoint is let out of reset while its
reference clock is still unconfigured, which is the reverse of what PCIe CEM
2.2 (PERST# Signal) asks for.

The MT7662 on port 1 of the TP-Link Archer XR500v tolerates it.  The MT7603 on
port 0 does not: the link trains, but the endpoint never completes a
configuration transaction, so it is never enumerated and the 2.4 GHz radio is
missing.

Bring the PHY up before the clock on the SoCs that need it, selected by a flag
in the SoC data so the other users of this driver keep the current order.  The
phy_init() and phy_power_on() calls further down become no-ops, both are
refcounted by the PHY core.

Tested on an Archer XR500v (EN7526G package, MT7603 on port 0, MT7662 on port
1) by TFTP-booting an initramfs on 6.18.41, two warm reboots and one cold power
cycle:

  pci 0000:01:00.0: [14c3:7603] type 00 class 0x028000 PCIe Endpoint
  mtk-pcie 1fb81000.pcie: port0 link retrained
  mt7603e 0000:01:00.0: ASIC revision: 76030010
  mt7603e 0000:01:00.0: firmware init done
  mt76x2e 0001:01:00.0: Firmware running!

Port 1 is unaffected, and port 0 trains at 0.66 s instead of 1.04 s, since the
vendor sequence no longer has to be run a second time after the PHY.

Signed-off-by: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 0b0b879108b7f91ba92215498f3fdfa3252ce8be)
---
 drivers/pci/controller/pcie-mediatek.c | 29 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-mediatek.c b/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-mediatek.c
index 538b3adf2..232969d2c 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-mediatek.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-mediatek.c
@@ -163,6 +163,7 @@ struct mtk_pcie_soc {
 	bool need_fix_class_id;
 	bool need_fix_device_id;
 	bool no_msi;
+	bool phy_before_sys_ck;
 	unsigned int device_id;
 	struct pci_ops *ops;
 	int (*startup)(struct mtk_pcie_port *port);
@@ -922,6 +923,33 @@ static int mtk_pcie_enable_port(struct mtk_pcie_port *port)
 	struct device *dev = pcie->dev;
 	int err;
 
+	/*
+	 * On the EN7528/EN751221 the "pcie" clock prepare callback ends by
+	 * deasserting PERST#, so preparing it before the port PHY is set up
+	 * lets the endpoint out of reset while its reference clock is still
+	 * unconfigured.  PCIe CEM 2.2 asks for the opposite order, and an
+	 * MT7603 on port 0 never answers configuration transactions when it
+	 * is brought up this way.  Set the PHY up first on those SoCs; the
+	 * phy_init()/phy_power_on() calls below are refcounted and become
+	 * no-ops.
+	 */
+	if (pcie->soc->phy_before_sys_ck) {
+		err = phy_init(port->phy);
+		if (err) {
+			dev_err(dev, "failed to initialize port%d phy\n",
+				port->slot);
+			return err;
+		}
+
+		err = phy_power_on(port->phy);
+		if (err) {
+			dev_err(dev, "failed to power on port%d phy\n",
+				port->slot);
+			phy_exit(port->phy);
+			return err;
+		}
+	}
+
 	err = clk_prepare_enable(port->sys_ck);
 	if (err) {
 		dev_err(dev, "failed to enable sys_ck%d clock\n", port->slot);
@@ -1345,6 +1373,7 @@ static const struct mtk_pcie_soc mtk_pcie_soc_mt7629 = {
 };
 
 static const struct mtk_pcie_soc mtk_pcie_soc_en7528 = {
+	.phy_before_sys_ck = true,
 	.ops = &mtk_pcie_ops_v2,
 	.startup = mtk_pcie_startup_port_en7528,
 	.setup_irq = mtk_pcie_setup_irq,
